index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..518d141db1190a4d74bdbebf3943c14c31260a7e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,203 @@
+# RAAG direct-product secret sharing (arXiv:1802.04870)
+
+> Status: the generator passes every local gate, but it is **not yet
+> shippable**. The required OpenRouter calls were attempted for the bare,
+> structural-hint, and placebo arms; every request was refused with HTTP 403
+> `Key limit exceeded`, before a model received the problem. G9 and the bare
+> hardening verdict therefore remain pending, not failed.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | rational exact |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate form | rational `[numerator, denominator]` |
+| Intended intuition | cyclic-translation and interpolation invariants |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed: Section 4, Theorem 4.1 and Proposition 4.2 |
+
+## What the family is
+
+The source is Flores, Kahrobaei, and Koberda,
+[*Algorithmic problems in right-angled Artin groups: complexity and
+applications*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04870). A right-angled Artin group
+(RAAG) has one generator per vertex of a graph, with commutation exactly along
+the graph edges. Each generated instance gives `d` exact cyclic RAAG
+presentations. If presentation `i` has `m_i` maximal direct-product factors,
+then an unknown monic degree-`d` polynomial satisfies `f(i)=m_i`. The solver
+returns the exact rational secret `f(0)`.
+
+This is the paper's own Section 6.3.1 sharing scheme. Theorem 4.1 identifies
+direct products with graph joins, and Proposition 4.2 identifies maximal join
+factors with connected components of the complement graph. The reduction is
+therefore paper-central and explicitly licensed, but the search is carried by
+the complement graph, so the profile conservatively says `licensed_reduction`
+rather than claiming fully native algebraic search.
+
+Generation is inverse: choose the factor counts first, then realize count `m`
+by a cyclic presentation on `N=mL` generators with noncommuting step `s=ma`,
+where `gcd(L,a)=1`. Its complement has exactly `gcd(N,s)=m` cycles. The checker
+recomputes every gcd and the exact binomial interpolation identity; it never
+reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Track A would be false. Proposition 4.2 explicitly gives a polynomial-time
+algorithm: build the complement and merge the endpoints of its edges. The
+included reference implementation literally runs that union-find algorithm on
+the implicit cyclic complement. At `easy`, seed 271828, it performs 2,136,676
+edge-merge attempts and takes 3.43 seconds; across eight audited seeds it solves
+8/8, using 14,916,968 merge attempts in 20.16 seconds.
+
+The compact route notices that translation by `s` partitions `Z/NZ` into
+`gcd(N,s)` orbits. After obtaining the `m_i`, subtracting the monic leading term
+turns the remaining problem into interpolation on consecutive integer nodes.
+The generator makes the count sequence constant except for sparse signed
+deviations, so only their binomial contributions survive. The measured route
+uses 178 exact operations on the shipping audit seed and at most 188 over the
+eight attack seeds. This is the intended no-tool gap: millions of routine merge
+attempts versus fewer than 200 exact operations if both invariants are seen.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+`make_instance(seed=0, n=7, degree=4, spikes=1, jitter=3)` renders the following
+complete instance (the general renderer additionally appends a hint only when
+`GV_HINT_MODE` requests one):
+
+```text
+Recover the exact secret in a right-angled Artin group sharing instance.
+
+A right-angled Artin group A(Gamma) has one generator for each vertex of
+a finite simple graph Gamma.  Two distinct generators commute exactly when
+their vertices are adjacent.  A join Gamma=Gamma_1*...*Gamma_r partitions
+the vertices into nonempty parts so every pair from different parts is
+adjacent.  A maximal join decomposition is one whose parts cannot be split
+again; its r parts give the maximal direct-product factors of A(Gamma).
+
+Below, presentation i has generators x_(i,0),...,x_(i,N-1).  All distinct
+pairs commute EXCEPT x_(i,a),x_(i,b) when (a-b) mod N equals s or N-s.
+Thus each line 'i N s' is a complete exact presentation, not a sample of
+relations.  Subscripts are zero-based and modular; the presentation lines
+may appear in any order.
+
+There is a unique monic polynomial f in Q[x] of degree exactly 4
+such that f(i)=m_i for i=1,...,d, where m_i is the number of maximal
+direct-product factors of presentation i and d is the displayed degree.
+Find the secret f(0).  Intervals and bounds below are inclusive.
+
+degree d = 4
+answer numerator bound B = 279
+presentation lines (i N s):
+  2 143 33
+  4 81 18
+  1 121 11
+  3 143 33
+
+Return one reduced exact rational num/den with gcd(|num|,den)=1,
+den>0, den exactly 1, and -B <= num <= B.  The JSON certificate stored
+by the generator is [num,den], but the answer block uses num/den text.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as num/den.
+Example: <answer>-37/1</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The component counts are `(11,11,11,9)`, and the secret is `37/1`. This demo is
+hand-solvable: four short Euclidean algorithms and the degree-four interpolation
+identity fit on paper.
+
+```python
+>>> verify(inst, [37, 1])
+(True, 'ok')
+>>> verify(inst, [38, 1])
+(False, 'incorrect polynomial secret')
+```
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Base cycle length | Degree / presentations | Sparse deviations | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 7 | 4 | 1 | hand example; never ships |
+| easy | 8,191 | 20 | 4 | provisional shipping level; oracle blocked |
+| medium | 16,381 | 24 | 5 | available, not reached |
+| hard | 32,767 | 28 | 6 | available, not reached |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="easy"` is provisional. No preset has been rejected; the
+oracle ladder could not complete its first attempt. `escalate()` doubles the
+ambient cycle length and jitter while preserving the two-atom answer.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses; all four presets and three seeds; JSON round-trip |
+| G2 | pass | 6/6 corruptions rejected with six distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged rational recovered through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; language size 4,865,804,016,388,931,551 |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; exactly one valid answer; demo count 1/559; reference cost 2,136,676 merge attempts |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference union-find 8/8; compact route 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled ambient size verifies; reference work rises 2,136,676 to 4,272,314; answer stays two atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 relabelling/reordering tests invariant and real; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **pending** | local caps pass: 23 chars, 2 atoms, 178 exact operations; oracle arms unavailable |
+
+The failing G6 attacks are: choose the factor count of the smallest
+presentation, use only the modal count, try 256 uniform bounded rationals, and
+perform the correct interpolation but stop after the first half of the shares.
+The last attack is deliberately in-context and uses much of the right idea; a
+hidden deviation in the second half makes it fail on every generated instance.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| Arm | Calls answered | Recorded requests | Result |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 | 4 | aborted: HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| structural hint | 0 | 4 | aborted: HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| placebo hint | 0 | 4 | aborted: HTTP 403 total key limit |
+
+There is no hinted-minus-placebo estimate and no hinted verdict. The structural
+hint only names cyclic translation invariance; it does not give `gcd`, the
+component count, an interpolation formula, or a derived quantity. The three
+transcripts are retained as infrastructure evidence, not counted as model
+failures. Once quota is restored, rerun the bare loop in this directory and the
+two G9 arms in separate scratch directories.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_1802_04870 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, parse_answer, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=12345, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+question = render(inst)
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>2432902009000000000/1</answer>")
+ok, reason = verify(inst, candidate)
+```
+
+After the oracle gates pass, emit from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1802.04870 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is emphatically Track B. A few seconds of union-find or a handful of gcd
+  calls solve it; there is no claim of distributional or complexity-theoretic
+  hardness.
+- G4 samples uniformly from the exact bounded rational language stated to the
+  solver. It does not model a generator-aware prior concentrated near `d!`, so
+  the factorial, modal-count, and truncated-interpolation probes separately test
+  that leakage. More sophisticated symbolic-sequence attacks were not tried.
+- The cyclic presentations are succinct. The measured reference cost expands
+  every complement edge as Proposition 4.2 prescribes; a software solver that
+  recognizes a circulant graph can use the same compact gcd route as the intended
+  solver. That is the Track B compression gap, not an omitted faster algorithm.
+- The answer happens to have denominator one because interpolation nodes are
+  `1,...,d`; it is still transported and checked as an exact reduced rational.
+- `canonical_key` is complete for this generated family—disjoint unions of equal
+  cycles are determined by their component count and cycle length—and is tested
+  for presentation reordering, cyclic-unit relabelling, reflection, and their
+  composition. The module does not accept arbitrary explicit edge-list encodings
+  of the same RAAG.
+- The current blocker is external quota. None of the twelve recorded oracle
+  requests reached a model, so they establish neither hardness nor easiness.
