   186	## Caveats
   187	
   188	- This is emphatically Track B. A few seconds of union-find or a handful of gcd
   189	  calls solve it; there is no claim of distributional or complexity-theoretic
   190	  hardness.
   191	- G4 samples uniformly from the exact bounded rational language stated to the
   192	  solver. It does not model a generator-aware prior concentrated near `d!`, so
   193	  the factorial, modal-count, and truncated-interpolation probes separately test
   194	  that leakage. More sophisticated symbolic-sequence attacks were not tried.
   195	- The cyclic presentations are succinct. The measured reference cost expands
   196	  every complement edge as Proposition 4.2 prescribes; a software solver that
   197	  recognizes a circulant graph can use the same compact gcd route as the intended
   198	  solver. That is the Track B compression gap, not an omitted faster algorithm.
   199	- The answer happens to have denominator one because interpolation nodes are
   200	  `1,...,d`; it is still transported and checked as an exact reduced rational.
   201	- `canonical_key` is complete for this generated family—disjoint unions of equal
   202	  cycles are determined by their component count and cycle length—and is tested
   203	  for presentation reordering, cyclic-unit relabelling, reflection, and their
   204	  composition. The module does not accept arbitrary explicit edge-list encodings
   205	  of the same RAAG.
   206	- The current blocker is external quota. None of the twelve recorded oracle
   207	  requests reached a model, so they establish neither hardness nor easiness.

