codex
Implemented and locally verified the Track B RAAG secret-sharing generator based on Sections 4 and 6.3.1 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1802.04870v3).

Files:

- [gen_1802_04870.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/gen_1802_04870.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1802.04870/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- G4: 0/200,000 valid random candidates.
- Reference algorithm: 2,136,676 merge operations at shipping size.
- Four attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Compact route: 178 exact operations.
- Answer: 23 characters, 2 atomic elements.
- Determinism, parsing, JSON round-trip, and corruption rejection verified.

The result is not yet shippable: all 12 required oracle requests were rejected before reaching a model with OpenRouter `HTTP 403: Key limit exceeded`. Consequently, G9 and the bare hardening verdict remain honestly pending. Restoring/increasing the OpenRouter key quota and rerunning `harden.py` is the only remaining work.
