+Track A would be false. Proposition 4.2 explicitly solves maximal join
+decomposition in polynomial time by forming the complement and merging the
+endpoints of every complement edge. Section 2 also records linear-time word and
+conjugacy algorithms and polynomial-time shortlex/geodesic algorithms; Section
+3 gives a quasi-polynomial automorphism consequence. Those are easy regimes,
+not hardness claims.
 
-The compact route notices that translation by `s` partitions `Z/NZ` into
-`gcd(N,s)` orbits. After obtaining the `m_i`, subtracting the monic leading term
-turns the remaining problem into interpolation on consecutive integer nodes.
-The generator makes the count sequence constant except for sparse signed
-deviations, so only their binomial contributions survive. The measured route
-uses 178 exact operations on the shipping audit seed and at most 188 over the
-eight attack seeds. This is the intended no-tool gap: millions of routine merge
-attempts versus fewer than 200 exact operations if both invariants are seen.
+The included reference implementation runs Proposition 4.2 literally after
+evaluating every Feistel-labelled edge. At provisional shipping `easy`, seed
+271828, it performs 327,680 edge iterations and 2,621,440 Feistel-round
+evaluations in 1.12 local seconds. Across the eight-seed G6 audit it solves 8/8
+and performs 23,592,960 counted operations in 13.19 seconds.
+
+The compact route notices that Feistel only renames vertices. Since `N` is a
+power of two and the hidden step multiplier is odd, `gcd(N,s)` is the lowest set
+bit of `s`. The remaining value is
+
+`f(0) = d! + sum_{i=1}^d (-1)^(i-1) C(d,i) m_i`
+
+for the even degrees used here. The full shipping audit needs at most 127 exact
+operations. The intended challenge is finding both invariants; millions of
+mechanical operations are easy for software but impossible in the no-tool
+context.
 
